TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "Pick and Roll (PNR)",
    "PNR success/failure",
    "Pull-up shot",
    "Offensive counter-strategy",
    "Offensive efficiency (Points Per Possession)",
    "Unit-level performance (player, team)",
    "League-wide trends"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define 'PNR fail' using available play-type data?",
    "How to identify 'PULL_UP' shots and their outcomes from play-by-play data?",
    "How to measure 'lift' in offensive efficiency (PPP) specifically for pull-ups *after* PNR failure?",
    "How to establish the temporal/causal link between PNR failure and subsequent pull-up?",
    "What constitutes a 'unit' (player, team, PNR combination) for analysis, and how to ensure sufficient sample size per unit?",
    "What statistical methods are appropriate to assess 'lift > 0 in >=70% of units with median lift > 2pp'?",
    "How to control for confounding factors like game state, specific defensive schemes, or player talent?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish any prior findings or limitations regarding this specific research question or related methodologies."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question requires a precise definition of 'PNR fail' and a clear temporal link between this failure and a subsequent 'PULL_UP' shot within the same possession.",
    "The 'lift' metric implies a comparison of Points Per Possession (PPP) for pull-ups after PNR failure against a suitable baseline, which needs to be explicitly defined.",
    "The definition of 'unit' (e.g., individual player, specific PNR initiator-screener combination, or team) will significantly impact sample size and the interpretation of 'league-wide counter'."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition of 'PNR fail' is not given and needs to be operationalized from the available 'play-type possession records'. Possible definitions include 0 points scored on the PNR action, a turnover, or a missed shot directly resulting from the PNR action.",
    "The exact method for identifying a 'PULL_UP' shot from 'action type' and 'sub-type' fields needs to be confirmed.",
    "The baseline for calculating 'lift' (e.g., player's average pull-up PPP, league average pull-up PPP, or PPP from other actions after PNR failure) is not specified.",
    "The specific definition of 'unit' for analysis (e.g., individual player, player-team combination) is not provided."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available, so it did not influence the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": []
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work on this specific question was retrieved."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "The specific sequence of 'PNR fail' immediately followed by a 'PULL_UP' shot within the same possession might be infrequent, leading to insufficient sample sizes for many 'units' (especially if 'unit' is defined granularly, e.g., per player or player combination). This could make it difficult to achieve statistical power for individual units or to identify a 'median lift > 2pp' across 70% of units."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Defining 'PNR fail' reliably and consistently across all possessions is critical. If the 'play-type possession records' do not explicitly mark 'PNR fail' and it needs to be inferred (e.g., from 0 points or a turnover), there's a risk of misclassification. Similarly, accurately identifying 'PULL_UP' shots from play-by-play action types requires careful specification to avoid including other jump shots."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Player talent, defensive scheme adjustments, game situation (score, clock), and other simultaneous offensive actions could confound the observed 'lift'. A player might resort to a pull-up after a PNR fail because they are a skilled pull-up shooter, or because the defense overcommits to stopping the PNR, creating an opening for a pull-up. It's difficult to isolate the 'counter-strategy' effect from these factors."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "The definition of 'lift' (PPP for pull-ups after PNR fail) must be carefully constructed relative to a suitable baseline. If the baseline itself is influenced by factors that correlate with PNR failure, the 'lift' might be an artifact. For example, if the baseline is general pull-up PPP, it might include situations where pull-ups are the primary, intended action, not a counter."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The condition 'lift>0 in >=70% of units with median lift>2pp' requires sufficient data points for each unit to reliably estimate 'lift' and its statistical significance. With a granular definition of 'unit' (e.g., individual player), many players might not have enough instances of 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' to meet this threshold, making it challenging to assess the 70% criterion."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The average difference in Points Per Possession (PPP) for pull-up shots taken immediately following a 'PNR fail' event on the same possession, compared to the player's baseline PPP on pull-up shots in other contexts, aggregated across units. The estimand is whether this 'lift' (difference) is > 0 for at least 70% of units, with a median lift across these units of > 2pp.",
    "unit": "An 'offensive unit' is defined as an individual player who attempts the pull-up shot. A secondary analysis may consider 'team' as a unit.",
    "sample": "All possessions from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (complete regular seasons and playoffs for all years, partial for 2025-26 as available) where a Pick and Roll (PNR) play type is recorded and subsequently leads to either a 'PNR fail' or a 'PNR success' event, and a pull-up shot is taken. Only players with a minimum of 50 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' possessions and 200 baseline pull-up possessions will be included as valid units for lift calculation.",
    "power": "A power analysis will be conducted to determine the minimum detectable effect size for 'lift' given the expected sample sizes per unit and across the league. We aim for 80% power to detect a median lift of 2pp if it exists, with alpha=0.05. If sufficient power cannot be achieved for a reasonable number of units, the study will report the limitations.",
    "controls": "For each 'unit' (player), 'lift' will be calculated as the difference between their PPP on 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' possessions and their overall PPP on pull-up shots that are not immediately preceded by a PNR fail within the same possession. This controls for individual player skill in pull-up shooting. Further controls for game state (score differential, quarter), opponent defensive scheme, and player fatigue (minutes played in game) will be explored using regression analysis if sample sizes permit. The definition of 'PNR fail' will be: a PNR play type possession that results in 0 points scored *from the PNR action itself* (e.g., turnover, missed shot not directly attributable to PNR success, shot clock violation, or other non-scoring outcome after the PNR action has resolved) and is immediately followed by a pull-up shot by the PNR initiator or another ball-handler on the same possession. The 'PULL_UP' shot will be identified by 'action type' and 'sub-type' fields in play-by-play data (e.g., 'Jump Shot' with 'Pullup Jump Shot' subtype).",
    "falsification": "A falsification test will investigate if 'lift' is observed for other non-pull-up shot types immediately following a PNR fail. If similar 'lift' is seen across multiple shot types, it would suggest a general offensive adjustment rather than a specific 'PULL_UP' counter. Additionally, analyze if the observed 'lift' for pull-ups after PNR fail is significantly higher than the lift for pull-ups after other types of offensive 'failure' (e.g., isolation fail, handoff fail).",
    "multiplicity": "Given the assessment across multiple 'units' and the '>=70% of units' criterion, a hierarchical modeling approach or false discovery rate (FDR) control (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg procedure) will be used to account for multiple comparisons when assessing the statistical significance of 'lift' for individual units. The primary metric (median lift across units) will be assessed as a single aggregate measure.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question is supported if, after controlling for player baseline pull-up efficiency, the estimated 'lift' in PPP for 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' possessions is statistically significant (>0) for at least 70% of the included player units, and the median of these individual unit 'lifts' is greater than 2.0 points per possession. If the median lift is <= 2.0pp or fewer than 70% of units show significant lift>0, the hypothesis is rejected. If insufficient units meet the sample size threshold for reliable lift calculation, the result will be deemed untestable.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 complete regular seasons and playoffs. NBA 2025-26 complete regular season and playoffs as available on 2026-06-21. All data from NBA stats API per-game files and derived play-type possession records."
  }
}

